From: pseudo <cdodge@pseudoseed.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 23:41:33 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 16/19] [Spec 250][Phase: phase_7] docs: the sidebar tree at
 390, 1440x900 and 1920
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Pictures of the page a human opens, at the three widths the criteria name, so
the appearance can be reviewed without running the stack. Produced by
`packages/codev/src/__tests__/e2e/spec-250-hierarchy.spec.ts` in the Codev
repository, against this tree's own web app and a server built from this source.

**There is no design reference for this tree.** The tiling geometry phase 9 ports
has numbers; the sidebar hierarchy has none. So these are not a comparison
against a mockup and must not be read as one — they exist so the appearance is
ruled on by a human against t3code's own sidebar conventions rather than assumed
from a green suite.

What the run measured, at every one of the three widths, rather than eyeballing:
no horizontal overflow (`documentElement.scrollWidth === clientWidth`), every
thread title at or above 13px, and zero console errors after pairing.

Two files per width. The page shot is the deliverable; the `-tree` shot is the
sidebar list at its full height, because the sidebar is its own scroll container
and a 900px window clips the tail — which is the orphan group, the part most
worth seeing.

Writing these is opt-in (`SPEC_250_WRITE_SCREENSHOTS=1`) and not tidiness:
`t3-server.mjs start-fork` refuses a dirty fork checkout, so a suite that wrote
new PNG bytes here on every run would pass once and skip forever, each run
leaving behind the modification that stops the next one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
